The big problem with this is that it's a well kept secret, inside and out.

On some models, a  special key sequence, on others, special connectors and
cable across the parrallel port and serial port and on others, who knows.

Generally, if the process is unknown, then one can do one of two things,

1. Go to Field Service, there may be a fee charged.
2. Take it apart, find the TOY (Time Of Year) clock chip, remove the regular
battery, remove the lithium backup battery or CMOS battery and in the case
of the Dallas TOY, short pins 4,5 and 20 together.

The second approach may or may not work, look up the device par number on
the web for the TOY chip and see what it's master reset sequence is and
follow that.

Good Luck.

rtt

P.S. Taking any LapTop PC apart is fraught with danger, ie; they are easily
damaged.

If you think that you have the skills and can save yourself a charge by
Field Service for resetting the password, fine. If you don't have the
skills, and you mangle the LCD display for instance, then you're out
probably $1200.00 USD in repairs. Think very carefully.






