I guess by your description that the printer you have is a (probably
non-DEC)
PARALLEL printer. (Centronics connector (36-'pin' connector) on the
printer). It
is NOT compatible with the SERIAL (DB-25) port on the system. If the
printer has
been used on a IBM compatible PC, it probably is parallel.

> Or should I connect it to the terminal (it's a
> DEC vt420 term) as there seems to be a socket with a printer icon next to
> the one that is being used to connect to the main box. I think it's
> aleady set up to
> handle a line printer, I think. I'm not sure what configuration file tells
> the system that a printer is connected someplace.

Again. This will be a SERIAL printer connector.

> 2. cd-rom: I'll need this to install anything decent on this machine
> (there is no tape drive). Besides being SCSI, are there any special
> requirements for a such a drive with this type of system, or will any old
> external cd-rom do? There's already a cdrom dir on /usr, so I guess things
> are partly set-up right.
 
I don't know if any SCSI CD-ROM drive will do, but (as Richard (Edward
Scissorhands) already stated) on DEC RRD-4?? model should work.

> 3. Modem: I'm guessing this hooks up to the smaller port on back. Any
> wierd stuff needed, or will an old external one do? I think one was
> hooked up to it one time, as there is a ttyd0 in /dev. Wermit is already
> installed.

The 25-pins DB connector (port 3) is used for modems. The signals on the
pins
should be the same as those on the large serial connector on any IBM
compatible
PC. The other, small lopsided phone jacks will be used for terminals.
 
> I know that these may sound like dumb questions, but I never really
> handled hardware stuff under a old-school unix system before. 

We probably all started out knowing nothing about these things we seem
to call
computers.

> Thanks.

You're welcome.

Alex.



