Ken Rachynski (krachyn@cadvision.com) wrote:
: Good day,
: 
: Because I am considered knowledgable in the realms of computers, my buddy 
: has given me two VAX machines that he purchased at a government auction. I 
: have been informed that the machines still contain the operating systems 
: intact so my job is to see if these boxes still work and whether it would 
: be worth it to attempt to resell them. My knowledge is pretty much 
: restricted to the Intel arena of computers and I'm at a loss to figure out 
: some things about these machines.
: 
: I did (finally) manage to get a cable wired up so I could connect my 
: computer to one of the two machines. Based on some documents I'm reading on 
: the web, I am in Monitor mode for the machine.
: 
: What I have is a VAXServer 3100 and a VAX 4000/300. I have succeeded in 
: connecting to the 3100 and have determined that it has a 209MB hard disk in 
: it. If I use the 'continue' command, I get the following output:
: 
: >>> continue
: 
: ?4F SCBINT
: 
:  83 BOOT SYS
: -DKA300
: ?42 NOSUCHFILE
:  84 FAIL
: 
: >>>

The CONTINUE command did continue the processor, however, the processor was
not likely pointing at valid code, nor were other things properly setup like
they should be at boot time, hence the ?4F.

The system then automatically tried to boot an operating system from DKA300
(SCSI target 3 on bus A), and since that disk did not appear to have a valid
operating system (NOSUCHFILE), the boot fails.

It's possible that there is an operating system on that disk, but that the
boot blocks are not set up properly, etc.  However, I'd bet that there's no
operating system on the disk.

Do a:

        >>> SHOW DEVICE

to see other disks that may be present and available.  If there are other
disks, try booting them:

        >>> BOOT DKA300 (or whatever SHOW DEVICE digs up)
        
        Stu



