I'm not a lawyer, but I believe that slander and libel require some factual
assertion that is refutable, or at least unsubstantiable.  Statements
clearly identifiable as personal opinions and the personal experiences that
led to them would not therefore qualify.

Proctor certainly appears to have made reasonable attempts to ascertain what
was going on, including attempts to contact islandco directly.  He then
advised others of his experiences and his conclusions, in an attempt to
perform a public service in a timely manner.  Furthermore, the circumstances
that led to this situation were the result of an islandco error, not
Proctor's.

As such, while I concede that he could have worded his warning more
carefully out of consideration for a company which might only have made an
error (as was the case here), I fail to see any real impropriety - save that
the response by islandco suggests a bullying corporate mind-set that I
personally find repugnant.

- bill

