In Article <80u514$tfr@usenet.pa.dec.com>
carlini@true.lkg.dec.com (Antonio Carlini) writes:
>In article <38326488.3B1C37F8@bessel.mpip-mainz.mpg.de>, Matthias Koch <koch@bessel.mpip-mainz.mpg.de> wrote:
>
>>Phillip Helbig wrote:
>>
>>>I tried a `Sony 90 P computer grade'.
>>
>>This is a 90m Tape, isn't it? The TZL04 can only handle 60m Tapes. I had
>>similar problems when using loger ones.
>
>It's worse than that, Jim ...
>
>Feed a TLZ04 anything other than 60m tapes and you are wearing the heads 
>significantly. I got the impression that doing it for only a short while would 
>destroy the heads.

Yes, the transport was never designed to handle the thinner tape in 90m
and longer tapes.  Too much tension, and the wrong contact angle with
the heads.

>As for eating tapes, my TLZ04s all seem to do this (mind you they are all old 
>now). I haven't found a cure but if I take the tapes out very carefully they 
>do seem to survive.

I looked into what causes the TLZ04 drives to mangle tapes when they get
old.  There is a tension roller that keeps the tape from being rewound
back into the cartridge.  Its pivot may need cleaning or lubing, or the
return spring attached to it may have lost tension.  Anyways, this leaves
a loop of tape out and when the drive lifts the cartridge and ejects it,
that gets caught.  It only gets worse with age.  I have a couple of dead
TLZ04 drives around here, was going to take one apart sometime and see if
they could be made to work again.  Just have not found the time yet.  I
hope this gives you something to look at to fix the drive.

Joe Heimann

heimann@ecs.umass.edu



