> Bob Kaplow (kaplow_r@eisner.decus.org) wrote:
> : In article
<50C24CAF5ED64B23.4EE0B6987752EA84.FCB377E2E6C51D18@lp.airnews.net>, Chris
Scheers <asi@airmail.net> writes:
> : > Can a wide StorageWorks cannister be used in a narrow box?  In
> : > particular, can I use a wide cannister in a BA353?
> :
> : NO!
> :
> : Narrow can be used in wide, but then all the devices on that bus run
narrow.
> : But wide can not be used in narrow.
>
> Well, almost right.
>
> You can NOT use wide devices in a BA350 (narrow) shelf, since the shelf
> grounds the upper 8 bits, and doesn't pass or terminate them anyway.
>
> You CAN use narrow devices in a BA356 (wide) shelf, provided the narrow
device
> is sufficient new.  Older narrow devices also grounded the upper 8 bits,
while
> the newer ones don't touch them at all.
>
> However, when using narrow devices in a wide shelf, each device will run
> narrow or wide, up to the limit of the controller.  Obviously, with a
narrow
> controller, all devices will run narrow.  But, with a wide controller,
each
> device will negotiate separately with the controller to determine whether
to
> run wide or narrow.
>
> Similarly for fast and slow devices.
>
>         Stu





