Good question. Following is a rant, er, commentary, from a recent issue of
my newsletter.

Operating systems live or die based on their apps portfolios. Based on this
metric, Compaq's OpenVMS OS may be surviving, but it sure ain't thriving.

And it's not just SKC's publisher or the extremely vocal OpenVMS base who is
making this assertion. Gartner Group senior IT analyst Tom Henkel recently
penned a research note entitled "Is It Still Safe to Invest in Alpha?"

Tom's message is unpleasant indeed for Compaq's OpenVMS management team and
for the OpenVMS customer base: "Third-party software availability is a more
significant short-term concern for OpenVMS customers than Alpha hardware
longevity. While OpenVMS customers could, in theory, continue to rely on
Alpha hardware through at least 2005, we continue to observe a significant
decline in packaged software availability for OpenVMS. OpenVMS customers
heavily dependent on third-party applications should immediately begin
exploring migration options with an objective of moving most key packaged
applications off the OpenVMS platform by the end of 2001."

The aforementioned "significant concern" is reflected in aggregate OpenVMS
revenues, which reportedly have declined more than 10 percent on a
year-over-year basis. It's also reflected in the OpenVMS applications
portfolio, which clearly is not in growth mode these days.

While roundly criticized as a marketing faux pas of the first order, Compaq'
s recent decision to scuttle AlphaNT could be a blessing in disguise for
OpenVMS. During the six years that NT was an option for Alpha desktops and
low-end servers, Compaq--and Digital before it--did precious little to
promote OpenVMS as a viable alternative to the Microsoft OS. On the
contrary, OpenVMS was constrained by an affinity strategy that never really
caught on in the customer base, and by pricing and positioning practices
that promoted NT at the expense of OpenVMS.

With NT removed from the Alpha equation, and with NT's enterprise-readiness
still very questionable, I submit that Compaq should take a different
approach. Under the AlphaNT trade-in program, Compaq will provide AlphaNT
users with gratis OpenVMS or Tru64 UNIX licenses. Alternatively, customers
can opt to downgrade from Alpha to a Wintel platform. Application
dependencies render Wintel the only choice for some customers, but.the
remainder of the AlphaNT base represents an attractive target for OpenVMS
deployment.

Given the AlphaNT Retreat, is there any reason why Compaq shouldn't offer
truly affordable low-end OpenVMS systems for developers and educators as
well as for application deployment? Such systems would help proliferate
Compaq's own architecture and OS rather than offerings from Mssrs. Gates and
Grove.

Affordability is critical. Compaq executives cite "total cost of ownership"
as justification for the current OpenVMS licensing model, but the sad fact
remains that the acquisition cost for OpenVMS-based systems places even
entry-level offerings beyond the reach of many customers. Sure, TCO is an
important consideration, but TCE (Total Cost of Entry) is the gating factor
at the low end.

Priced more competitively with Wintel, low-end OpenVMS solutions might
thrive. ISVs might be more willing to deploy on the OS, and customers might
be more likely to belly up to the OpenVMS bar. While there's nothing certain
about this "what-if" scenario, it seems far more palatable than the
inevitable result of a "stay-the-course" strategy.




What baffles me about the Q's OpenVMS pricing and positioning strategy is
that the firm would rather sell someone else's OS and chip at very low (two
to three percent) margins instead of selling its own OS and chip at a
somewhat higher margin. Could it be yet another instantiation of Microsoft's
"Hypocritic Oath?"*

*First, do no harm... to Microsoft or its business interests!


Terry Shannon





