MOP DL 8:0:2b:d:b2:f   > aa:0:4:0:1:dc     len    3 code 0a RML 
MOP DL aa:0:4:0:1:dc     > 8:0:2b:d:b2:f   len  122 code 02 MLD
MOP DL 8:0:2b:d:b2:f   > aa:0:4:0:1:dc     len    3 code 0a RML
MOP DL aa:0:4:0:1:dc     > 8:0:2b:d:b2:f   len   32 code 14 PLT
MOP DL 8:0:2b:d:b2:f   > aa:0:4:0:1:dc     len    3 code 0a RML

ab:0:0:1:0:0 is some sort of DEC-defined broadcast address (I think).
What happens is the VAX broadcasts, looking for a server that
has an image for 8:0:2b:d:b2:f (the first RPR).  The Linux box responds 
with "I've got one!" (the ASV).  The VAX then says directly 
(non-broadcast) "Gimme my image" (the second RPR).  Then we have 
MLD/RML pairs as the image is transferred over followed by a PLT/RML
pair to finish it off.

>i have tried two copy's of the mopd package, modified the code in loop.c,
