In article <382C244D.78B34BFA@ira.uka.de>,
Stepanov  <stepanov@ira.uka.de> wrote:
>I just though, that something already exists and may be it is fourth method.
>But I have ROM contents of this modules and I need to understand behaviour of
>driver units. If somewhere exists some emulator and debuger for PDP11, it would
>be good for me to find it.

There's a Demo version of the Ersatz-11 emulator which you can download from
www.dbit.com.  It has some built-in debugging features (disassembler,
single stepping, proceed w/breakpoint) which may be useful to you.

As for bus adapters, there are several options.  There's the H&L UPG3600-PIO
board, which connects ISA bus to Unibus.  E11 (commercial version) currently
supports it only for programmed I/O (still haven't gotten that NPR pseudo-DMA
interrupt handler working!), however it's very straightforward to program
(basically it just gives you raw control over the Unibus lines so you do all
the bus protocol in software, including NPRs) so it would be the easiest to
get working with your own PC program.

Then there are the BCI-2003 and BCI-2103 boards from The Logical Company, which
bridge PCI to Unibus or Q-bus, and are much more automatic boards.  That plus
the fact that they're PCI boards makes them kind of a pain to initialize
(you need to map a physical address window to reach the memory-mapped
registers), and they're picky about DMA (the 4 MB PDP-11 memory region has
to be physically contiguous, start on an 8 KB boundary, and not span the 2
GB boundary).  E11 (full version) supports this but only when running its own
memory manager, since none of the DPMI servers out there seems to support the
"lock DMA region" VDS call properly.  If your PC program runs in DOS (or a DOS
session in Windows or OS/2) you'll have the same problem, unless you don't do
Q/Unibus DMA, or if you can restrict your Q/Unibus DMA to the low 4 KB of the
bus address space (i.e. before the first "fold" in your memory buffer due to
paging).

Bit3 (now SBS Bit3) *used* to sell Q-bus adapter boards but they've
been discontinued (unless you want to buy ten at once, at full price).
I had no luck with their PCI/Q product (it didn't work even with their own
sample software), but I believe they did actually support EISA at one point.
So maybe you could scare something up used.  My understanding is, all of their
products actually convert to their own private bus to go between boards,
so in theory you can mix & match any two busses as long as you don't mind
writing all your own software.

Anyway, pointers to both H&L and Logical are on www.dbit.com.  Q/Unibus
adapters for PCs are not high-volume products so be prepared for the prices!
They're totally worth it if you really need them though.

John Wilson
D Bit



