Nick,

You simply need another 90MHz CPU and an operating system that
supports dual processors such as Windows NT or Linux.  Maybe....

Open up the case of the computer with it on its side so you are
looking down on the motherboard.  DEC calls a motherboard a "main
logic board".  With the front of the computer facing you, look along
the left hand edge of the motherboard for numbers inscribed on the
surface of the board.  The DEC motherboard part numbers all have 54
series numbers in the DEC scheme of things.  If the motherboard part
number is 54-23606, you are all set.  Nothing else to do.

If the motherboard part number is anything else (a lower number), you
won't be able to run a dual processor setup without replacing the main
logic board.

FYI, the hardware supports up thru a 133MHz Pentium as currently set
up.  With the replacement of another part on the processor card, it'll
run a 200Mhz CPU, the limit for which it was designed.  I have also
heard that a 233MHz Pentium MMX with a PowerLeap adapter runs well.

... Ben Myers

