koen <klibbrecht@roularta.be> writes:
> Does anyone has an idea of a problem with two digital nics namely the
> FR-PCTBZ-AA and the DE500 in high load conditions?  It seems that the link
> between the nic and the switch (a bay 450T switch) drops every two seconds.
> We have made an electric split between nic and switch by two mediaconverters
> via fiber to keep up the link at the side of the switch but now we see that
> the links is dropped at the site of the card.  I changed the original card
> FR-PCTBZ-AA by the DE500 and even switch the server 7300 by a 4000  to avoid
> any other hardware problems without any improvements.  The only thing that
> is similar in all situations is the nic-controllerchip wich is the digital
> 21140.  We get the problem in high load conditions (yes we tried setting the
> 100Mb speed at full duplex fixed or half duplex fixed etc...)

  You don't say what platform you're using, so it could well be a driver
problem.

  I have some systems that output about 1/4 terabyte/day each on assorted
DE500 cards (DE500-XA pass 1.2, DE500-BA, and a 3rd-party card with a 21140A
pass 2.2 on it). They don't give me any problems.

  For example, this box has been up for an hour and 22 minutes and has sent
out 15.5 million packets:

[0:19] news:~> netstat -i
Name  Mtu   Network       Address            Ipkts Ierrs    Opkts Oerrs  Coll
de0   1500  <Link>      00.06.2b.01.1d.fa  9800495     0 15579019     3     0
de0   1500  165.254       news             9800495     0 15579019     3     0
lo0   16384 <Link>                               0     0        0     0     0
lo0   16384 127           localhost              0     0        0     0     0
[0:20] news:~> uptime
11:05AM  up  1:22, 1 user, load averages: 0.74, 0.74, 0.75

  The 3 oerrs are the FreeBSD (3.3-STABLE) driver deciding that it needs to
adjust its FIFO threshold:

de0: abnormal interrupt: transmit underflow (raising TX threshold to 96|256)
de0: abnormal interrupt: transmit underflow (raising TX threshold to 128|512)
de0: abnormal interrupt: transmit underflow (raising TX threshold to 160|1024)

