Well, I've recieved my Tru64 kit this week (Monday), and can shed some light
on what it includes.

- Seems to be the same media as an actual Commercial Tru64 5.0 kit
- Includes documentation CD as well as the Open Source Internet solutions CD
- Licenses for:
   - Base OS (OSF-BASE)
   - User (OSF-USR)
   - Server extensions (OSF-SVR)
   - Networker Save & Restore
   - (2) seats for Advanced Server
   - Developer's toolkit (OSF-DEV)

I think that's it, although one may have slipped my mind...

I'll be installing this weekend hopefully, perhaps I can shed some more
light then...

FWIW, the $99 looks well spent.  I've been running 4.0 at home for a while
now, using some extra licenses from work, and the Non-commercial kit seems
to cover everything I'm used to having except perhaps Open3d, which I think
has become a non-licensed product for 5.0.  Basically, it's a good,
well-rounded package from what I can see.

Oh, and if you're used to any of the free Unix variants, if you've got the
cash to spare, give Tru64 a try.  I've worked fairly extensively now with
most of the major commercial Unices, and started with the free OS world, and
would rank Tru64 as number 1 on my list.  Very stable, very full-featured,
runs well, Alpha hardware is FAST and inexpensive, all-around a good thing!
My Alpha(a 250 4/266) sits next to an HP 9000 B132L, a Sun Sparc 5, and an 
SGI Indigo2, and it's probably my favorite out of the bunch, except for that 
fact that I find myself working with HP-UX these days more due to the current 
customer site I'm working at...

And I do like OpenVMS as well, but it's likely to be the OS on the second
hard drive in my Alpha, and not the other way around ;)

Chris

