Homonnai Mihaly <homer@unicorn.sch.bme.hu> writes:

> Chris Tribo <t1345@hopi.dtcc.edu> wrote:
> : Thank you! Yes, that helps very much. 
> : 	My LB has BNC and AUI on Port A and a DEPCI-AA AUI card in Port B. I
> : will have it setup with the DS and VS connected by BNC on port A. I have
> : a Micronet AUI to 10BaseT transceiver on port B. While I am on the
> : topic, what does the SQE switch on the transciever do? I have always
> : seen it in the off position on the transcievers I've picked up.
> 
> You have to switch SQE off. SQE means Signal Quality Error (if i
> remember).

While Signal Quality Error is the correct name, what is disabled is
SQE Test, not SQE. SQE is the proper name for the collision signal
from the transceiver to the host. SQE Test is often called "heartbeat'
and is used to assure that the collision detection hardware in the
transceiver is working.

The SQE switch should be on for a bridge connection. It only should be
off for connection to a repeater. (Though, in practice, it really
matters little.) If you have management software, you will see an error
for every packet sent if the SQE Test is disabled on a LB200
transceiver, but it still works. (Also, if you have the ELMS or RBMS
software, you can configure the LB200 to ignore this error.)

> clearVision software can't handle tehese old bestias. I think they are
> manageable using old DEC software like DECmcc. However you can read some
> counters using these old SW, so there are no filters, or other things you
> could manage. It is an old dumb hardware.

Not true. While the LB100 was pretty dumb, the 200 has full filtering
capability. There are also spanning tree and timing parameters that
are configurable, even on a 100.
