In article <slrn81tg4a.3gp.power@Zoot.ss.pub.ro>,
Daniel PAVEL <power@kermit.ss.pub.ro> wrote:

>	A short newbie question, if you don't mind..
>	How do I netboot a MivcroVAX II?  It has 5MB of memory,
>a TK50 tape drive, and two 71MB RD53 disks (I'm telling you all this
>in case you might make out what model it is, because I couldn't figure
>it out -- it doesn't say anywhere on it, and it has no papers or
>documentation whatsoever)..  Basically, the disks have failed, so the
>machine can no longer boot from them, but as far as I understood,
>they'd still be usable.. so I'd like to netboot from another MV II
>(another model, it's about four times the size of the first :) )
>that runs NetBSD.  I've managed to setup the bootp server, but have
>no idea of how to tell the first machine to try booting from
>the local net..

As others have mentioned, the ethernet device on the MicroVAX II is XQA0
so you enter "B XQA0" at the >>> prompt.  For details on netbooting
setups, you should probably check out my Netbooting HOWTO:

  http://world.std.com/~bdc/projects/vaxen/VAX-netboot-HOWTO.html

Apologies in advance for it being rather dated, but it should give you
some decent clues about what's required to netboot your MicroVAX II.
Netbooting is now supported for pretty much all the VAX platforms which
are capable of running NetBSD/vax (as of the 1.4.1 release).

Apart from running bootpd or dhcpd, you'll need to be running rarpd and
mopd.  The mopd daemon is needed to get the bootloader across to the VAX. 
Once the bootloader kicks in, it pulls information from your BOOTP, DHCP,
or BOOTPARAMS server.   You'll also need the NFS server daemons running,
and a handful of /dev/bpf* devices.

-brian.
