It is not SNMP manageable.
I've a LB200 with an AUI - AUI/BNC connectors, and my DIP setting is:
12345678
UUDDUDDD
Where U-switch is in UP position, D-switch is DOWN position. It works
fine, when i'm using the AUI connector on both side.

: NVRAM, general information, docs, or software on it?
:     I get errors from my DS and my VS on boot up, the DEC reports 6/ext-lb
: [PMAD-AA] (3: xmt [0x1, 0x2] cable) when I have a BNC cable running from the
: Dec to the LANBridge or when it is not connected. When I try to configure
: le0 on the DS I always get device timeout or it responds no carrier and will
: not broadcast an IP.
:     The VS error codes are too long and cryptic for me to remember other
: than that one began with E, another 1 and another C. I don't have a
: terminator to cap it off with so I don't know if that is the issue or not.
:     I'm also quite new to 10Base2, I honestly know nothing about it other
: than it can run about 180 yards as opposed to about 100 for 10BaseT. So I

The BNC can be as far as 185m long. However i had an about 300m long
segment, and it worked fine.

: have a few questions if someone could be kind enough to answer:

: Does 10Base2 use regular ole' cable TV wire with BNC connectors? (78 ohm) or
: must it be lower/higher? The BNC cables I have been trying read about 78
: ohms.

The 10base2 uses 50ohm cable, with 50ohm terminators.

: Can you connect two computers with 10Base2 directly together with no
: additional hubs/converters and can I connect the DS or VS to the LANBridge
: without extra hubs/converters?

You can connect the two machines together using two T-connectors, and two
50ohm terminators:
[]-T----------------T-[]
   M1               M2

Where the [] terminator is directly attached to the T-connector, and
machines M1 and M2 are directly connected to the T-connector.

What kind of connectors does the LB200 have? Two BNC?
Then you can connect the machines to the rest of the 10base2 network this
way:
        net1
[]-T------T----------T-[]
   M1     M2         LB200   net2
                      []T---------rest of the 10base2 network

So in this case you have two collision domains, so local traffic between
the M1 and M2 machines remainds on the net1 network, while local net2
traffic remains on the net2 network. Only broadcast messages reache both
of the networks.
If you have AUI connectors (15 pin), you need external transceivers to
connect to the 10base2 network:

[]-T-----T-------T-[]
   M1    M2      tr
                 |
                 |AUI cable
                 |
                LB200
                 |AUI cable
                 |
                 tr
               []T--------- rest of the 10base2 network

tr means transceiver.
The AUI cable can be 50m long.

: Are 10Base2 terminators passive resistors, if so, how many ohms?

Yes, they are passive, and 50ohm.
: Do 10BaseT and 10Base2 have about the same throughput in practice? (None of
: this theoretical stuff :-)

Yes, both are 10Mbit/second network. The main difference is the topology.

Hope this help.
Mihaly



