From: clc5q@cobra.cs.Virginia.EDU (Clark L. Coleman)
Subject: Alpha 21164 performance counters
Date: 1999/11/04
Message-ID: <7vsd7m$r0g$1@murdoch.acc.Virginia.EDU>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 544450287
Organization: University of Virginia Computer Science Department
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


I am doing performance experiments on a DEC Personal Workstation 600a
running OSF/1 V4.0. Using the pseudo-device hpm to access the performance
counters, I was examining the Data TLB Misses. As I read and re-read
portions of a large array, in ever larger chunks, I see close to zero
Data TLB Misses on the re-read up through 48 * 8KB  (48 entries of 8KB
page size each.) Jumping up from 48 to 56 * 8KB, the data TLB starts
thrashing on the re-read, indicating that the first read of the 56
pages are not left in the TLB (started evicting each other before 56th
entry.)

All documentation I can get on the Alpha 21164 says that it has 64
fully associative entries in the data TLB, and 48 in the instruction
TLB. The miss counters indicate 48 entries in the Data TLB, not 64.

Questions:

1) Anyone know for sure what the DTLB entries are?

2) Anyone know for sure if OSF/1 nails down 16 entries for OS use,
such as I/O page direct mapping or whatever? Or any other restriction
that makes non-privileged user code only have access to 48 entries?

Thanks for any info.

Clark Coleman
University of Virginia




