If you can cobble up a cable to permit using two drives at a time, you
might find it worth while to remove the top plate on the Micropolis
and 'encourage' it to let go and boot up.  Then do a disk to disk copy
to the other drive.  

I believe that most any MFM drive will work, but the largest that I
know of is the Maxtor XT-2190 which is about 160mb formatted.  That
corresponds to DEC RD54.  I suspect that if a replacement drive
corresponds to a DEC RDnn in geometry that it will be recognized and
format easily.  There is a procedure under T 70 (I think that is
right) to format 'foreign' drives, but it involves inputting a lot of
drive data that is not readily available.  

