Organization: University of Maryland
Reply-To: bleau@umdsp.umd.edu
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms,vmsnet.alpha

I had a problem similar to this a while back, and our FE gave me a (sort of)
answer.

The theory put forth was that, yes, the drive/cpu should negotiate narrow
transfer mode, but that assumes the two parties *know about* the item being
negotiated.  If the drive says it can do narrow or wide, but the cpu (more
exactly, the SCSI controller in the system box), since it is so old, has
absolutely no idea what "narrow" or "wide" mean, it could very well ignore the
disk altogether.

I have no solution for you, though.  In my case, we put the disk on a more
recent system, and it worked fine, so we used it there and accessed it over the
net.  Maybe someone else can confirm this theory, or poke a hole in it.  For
me, it sounded close enough to enable me to close out the problem and go with a
workaround.


